  "source_path": "/scratch/ziv_baretto/Thesis_Ziv/Capstone-Thesis-/DATA_SET_BUILDER_AND_EXPLORER/Timeline_Maker/output_merged_v3/food_safety/Sushil Kumar vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh Thr on 11 May, 2017.json",
  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sushil Kumar vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh Thr on 11 May, 2017 -( 1 )- M.Cr.C.No.12760/2015 High Court Of Madhya Pradesh Bench At Gwalior Single Bench: (Vivek Agarwal, J.) M.Cr.C.No.12760/2015 .....Petitioner : Sushil Kumar Versus .....Respondents : State of M.P. & Anr. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------",
    "facts": "2. It is petitioner's case that he is being prosecuted in R.C.C. No.1694/2007 in the Court of JMFC, Morena, for an alleged offence punishable under Section 7 read with Rule 50 punishable under Section 16 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (in short \"the Act of 1954\") and Food Adulteration Rules, 1955 (in short \"the Rules of 1955\") and the evidence before charge is yet to commence. It is petitioner contention that he was visited by respondent No.2 on 8.5.2000 and sample of edible Ghee was taken and was sent to public analyst and on the basis of report of the public analyst a complaint was filed on 28.8.2000 against the petitioner by respondent No.2 without any information to the petitioner. According to the petitioner, he appeared before the Court on 27.7.2004 and after furnishing bail bonds, he had filed an application under Section 13(2) of the Act of 1954 before the Court which could not be considered due to non-filing of reply by the prosecution for a period of about two years. On 26.6.2006 said application was allowed on the basis of no objection of the prosecution and petitioner was directed to deposit a sum of Rs.1,000/- as fee to send the sample for analysis by the Central Food Laboratory. It is his case that he had deposited the requisite amount, but since there was lapse of six years in between the sample was taken from the petitioner and its deposit for testing before the Central Food Laboratory, it had become futile exercise, therefore, he had moved an application under Section 245(2) of Cr.P.C. before the Court of Sushil Kumar vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh Thr on 11 May, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/22288834/ 1 JMFC, Morena, for discharge in the light of various circulars of Central Food Laboratory which prescribes that such stale sample cannot be tested and no useful purpose is going to be served by testing of such sample. On the basis of such submission, it is prayed that since he was never intimated about the report from the public analyst, therefore, placing reliance on several decisions of this Court as well as Supreme Court, a prayer has been made for quashing of the proceedings in R.C.C. 1694/07 pending before the JMFC, Morena.",
    "arguments": "It is mentioned that Local Health Authority shall within a period of ten days after institution of prosecution forward a copy of the report of the result of analysis in Form III delivered to him under sub-rule (3) of rule 7, by registered post or by hand, as may be appropriate, to the person from whom the sample of the article was taken by the Food Inspector and simultaneously also to the person, if any, whose name, address and other particulars have been disclosed under Section 14A of the Act.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It is mentioned that Local Health Authority shall within a period of ten days after institution of prosecution forward a copy of the report of the result of analysis in Form III delivered to him under sub-rule (3) of rule 7, by registered post or by hand, as may be appropriate, to the person from whom the sample of the article was taken by the Food Inspector and simultaneously also to the person, if any, whose name, address and other particulars have been disclosed under Section 14A of the Act."
  },
